 ## Operating characteristics
 - **Time Complexity:** Normal computation is $O(1)$ per vertex/sample. Shading is $O(1)$ per fragment.
-- **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory for normals if computed on the fly [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory for normals if computed on the fly [S2].
 - **Measured Performance:** Not specified in sources.
-- **Strengths:** Consistent visual appearance across different rendering techniques (rasterization and ray-marching) [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on central difference normal estimation, which may be sensitive to noise or boundaries [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Consistent visual appearance across different rendering techniques (rasterization and ray-marching) due to identical fragment shader implementation [S2][S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Relies on central difference normal estimation, which requires neighboring voxels and may produce undefined normals in flat regions [G]. Boundary handling is not specified in the sources [G].
 
 ## Background
-Gooch Shading was originally proposed by Gooch et al. in 1998 [GGSC98], cited in [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is used in volume rendering to provide illustrative shading that enhances shape perception [S1][S2, unofficial]. The specific application with central difference normals in rasterization is described in [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Gooch Shading was originally proposed by Gooch et al. in 1998 [S1][S2]. It is used in volume rendering to provide illustrative shading that enhances shape perception by mapping orientation to warm and cool tones [S1][S4, unofficial]. The specific application with central difference normals in rasterization is described in [S2][S3, unofficial].
 
